  <p>The ambient lighting inside L'Eclat was designed to make everyone look like a softer, kinder version of themselves. It was a luxury Officer Vance rarely experienced. Sitting across from Elara Maxwell, dressed in a charcoal suit that felt uncomfortably restrictive compared to his usual leather jacket, he felt entirely out of his depth.</p>

<p>Elara, by contrast, fit into the velvet-and-crystal aesthetic seamlessly. Yet there was a sharp edge to her tonight, a precision in the way she swirled her wine that reminded him, just slightly, of the clinical stillness he had encountered earlier that day.</p>

<p>"Three weeks, Officer Vance," Elara said, setting her glass down with a soft clink. She tilted her head, a challenging but amused smile playing on her lips. "You leave a woman on read after she sends you a piece of her mind, a poem no less, and then you suddenly resurface with an invitation to the most exclusive restaurant in the district. Should I be flattered, or should I be looking for a hidden agenda?"</p>

<p>Officer Vance rubbed the back of his neck, a rare, genuine flush creeping up his collar. "You should be insulted, honestly. I don't have an excuse that makes me look good, Elara. The truth is... the city has been bleeding. I've been living out of my car and breathing precinct coffee for twenty-one days straight."</p>

<p>Elara's expression softened, her curiosity piqued. "The Thorne murder? It's all the tabloids are talking about. They're calling the killer a phantom."</p>

<p>"They're calling him Arin," Officer Vance muttered, leaning forward, his eyes darkening as the pressure of the case seeped into his voice. "He's a ghost. Leaves no thermal signature, no DNA, just a monomolecular wire and a goddamn flower. I spent the afternoon questioning his prime suspect, and then..." He trailed off, shaking his head. "I had a bizarre encounter with the suspect's therapist."</p>

<p>"Oh?" Elara adjusted her posture, resting her chin on her hand. "A therapist in the Sink? That sounds like a masochistic profession. Who is he?"</p>

<p>"Maxwell," Officer Vance said, slicing into his steak. "Dr. Tirian Maxwell. The guy is like a block of ice. I've never met anyone so intensely... still. It was like talking to a statue that knew exactly what I was going to say before I said it."</p>

<p>Elara froze. Her breath caught in her throat for a fraction of a second before a bright, defensive laugh escaped her lips.</p>

<p>"Tirian? You interviewed my brother?"</p>

<p>Officer Vance dropped his fork. It clattered loudly against the porcelain.</p>

<p>"Tirian Maxwell is your brother?"</p>

<p>"The very same," Elara said, her eyes gleaming with protective amusement. "And he isn't a block of ice, Officer Vance. He's an anchor. He's the most predictable, stable, and entirely safe man in this chaotic city. He lives his life by a stopwatch."</p>

<p>"Predictable isn't the word I'd use," Officer Vance murmured, staring at his plate as a faint shiver ran down his spine. "He gave me a psychological profile of this Arin character that completely rattled me. He told me Arin isn't a loud man. He called him a ghost of cold, geometric necessity. He told me that if I wanted to find the killer, I should stop looking in the alleys and start looking for the quietest room in the city."</p>

<p>Hearing her brother's clinical, hyper-precise words repeated by a detective over dinner sent an involuntary chill through Elara's veins. She forced a smile, trying to shake off the sudden heaviness in the air.</p>

<p>"Well... Tirian has always had a way of making reality sound like a diagnosis."</p>

<div style="text-align: center; margin: 2rem 0;">♦️ ♦️ ♦️</div>

<p>Miles away, the air inside Tirian Maxwell's private office was perfectly temperature-controlled and smelled faintly of eucalyptus. The ticking of a vintage grandfather clock was the only sound breaking the silence.</p>

<p>Tirian sat in his high-backed velvet chair, his fountain pen hovering over his leather-bound notebook. Across from him sat Julian, the wealthy investor, his face pale and his hands trembling violently as he clutched a glass of water.</p>

<p>"I can't sleep, Doc," Julian stammered, his voice cracking with panic. "Ever since Mark Thorne was found... with that thing in his throat. I was at L'Eclat the night before, discussing my shipments to Country X. What if this Arin is targeting anyone connected to the Thorne gala? What if I'm next?"</p>

<p>Tirian watched the man's erratic breathing with clinical detachment. He adjusted his glasses, his low-frequency voice acting as a steady, hypnotic weight in the quiet room.</p>

<p>"Fear, Julian, is an irrational projection of future variables. You are conflating proximity with intent. Tell me about the shipments. Why do you believe your financial ventures make you a target for an artist of termination?"</p>

<p>As Julian launched into a frantic, disjointed explanation of his underworld logistics, Tirian listened intently, nodding slowly. He was entirely present, a dedicated physician absorbing the fractured psyche of a desperate patient.</p>

<div style="text-align: center; margin: 2rem 0;">♦️ ♦️ ♦️</div>

<p>Outside the glowing facade of L'Eclat, the rain began to fall in thin, silver needles. The street was quiet, save for the distant hum of the city's lower sectors. Officer Vance's unmarked police cruiser sat parked beneath a flickering neon sign, its windshield slick with water.</p>

<p>From the deep, absolute shadows of the adjacent alleyway, a figure detached itself from the darkness.</p>

<p>It moved with an effortless, silent grace that seemed to defy the heavy downpour, a phantom walking through the upper crust of the city. The reader could see him clearly now: Arin. The rogue assassin, wrapped in a dark, tailored coat, his face obscured by the low tilt of his hat. While the police scrambled to lock up high-society scapegoats, the true monster of the Sink was walking free, stalking his hunter.</p>

<p>Arin stepped up to the driver's side of Vance's cruiser. He didn't break the glass. He didn't force the lock. Instead, with a slow, deliberate movement of his gloved hand, he slid something beneath the heavy rubber of the windshield wiper.</p>

<p>A single, sharp Veridian Thorn, its dark green stem glinting beneath the neon light, accompanied by two blood-red petals.</p>

<p>Arin lingered for a moment, staring at the restaurant's glass doors, where Officer Vance and Elara were laughing over dessert. Then, like a glitch in the light, the shadow stepped backward and vanished completely into the misty night.</p>

<div style="text-align: center; margin: 2rem 0;">♦️ ♦️ ♦️</div>

<p>The date ended on a lingering, electric note. Officer Vance walked Elara to a waiting hover-cab, his hand resting gently on the small of her back.</p>

<p>"I apologise if I brought too much of the precinct to dinner," Vance said, his rugged face softening as he looked down at her. "I usually don't get distracted like this."</p>

<p>"It's fine, Officer Vance," Elara smiled, stepping into the cab. "A little danger keeps the system alive. Just... try not to lock my brother up. He wouldn't survive the lack of premium tea at the station."</p>

<p>Vance laughed, watching her cab lift into the neon-soaked sky before turning back towards his cruiser.</p>

<p>The smile died on his face.</p>

<p>Even from ten feet away, he could see it. The dark green stem. The blood-red petals.</p>

<p>Vance's blood turned to ice. He drew his weapon instantly, spinning around as his eyes searched the empty, rain-slicked street. There was no one. Just the sound of the rain and the distant hum of traffic.</p>

<p>The killer had been there. While Vance was eating dinner and laughing with Elara, Arin had been standing right outside. The phantom wasn't just running; he was watching. He was targeting Vance.</p>

<div style="text-align: center; margin: 2rem 0;">♦️ ♦️ ♦️</div>

<p>When Elara entered the Maxwell penthouse, the lights were dimmed to a low, ambient glow. Tirian was sitting in his high-backed chair by the floor-to-ceiling windows, staring out at the sprawling, vertical labyrinth of the city. His notebook was closed on his lap, his posture perfectly erect.</p>

<p>"You're still awake," Elara said softly, kicking off her heels. "I thought you'd be asleep by your scheduled time."</p>

<p>"Julian's session ran late. His anxiety required a more... intensive recalibration," Tirian replied, his voice maintaining that soothing, mechanical rhythm. He didn't turn around. "How was your evening, Elara?"</p>

<p>Elara walked over, leaning against the glass window near him.</p>

<p>"I had dinner with Officer Vance. It turns out he's the detective on the Thorne case. He thinks you're fascinating, Tirian... and a little terrifying."</p>

<p>Tirian finally turned his head, his expression a flawless, vacant mask of mild sibling curiosity.</p>

<p>"Terrifying is simply a word people use for things they cannot calculate, Elara. Did the detective enjoy his dinner?"</p>

<p>"He did," Elara murmured, looking out at the rain. "But he's obsessed. He thinks Arin is watching him. He thinks the killer is playing a game with the police."</p>

<p>Tirian looked back out at the city, his eyes reflecting the cold, distant neon lights.</p>

<p>"The police see games where there is only design, Elara. Sleep well. Tomorrow, the city will have a completely new set of variables."</p>

